\chapter{The Command Line Becomes a Language}
\label{ch:tools}
\index{shell}\index{pipes}\index{McIlroy, Douglas}

\section{Commands as ordinary programs}

A command line looks like a place to type instructions to the operating
system. In Unix, most instructions typed there are requests to an ordinary
program, the shell. The shell interprets syntax, expands names, arranges
input and output, starts programs, and collects their status. Keeping this
work outside the kernel made the command language replaceable without
changing the machinery that protects files and schedules processes.

That distinction explains why Unix could acquire several shells. Thompson's
early shell, Stephen Bourne's shell, Bill Joy's C shell, the Korn shell,
and later Bash and others embody different priorities. An interactive user
may value history and completion; a script author may value predictable
control flow and portability. A shell can serve both audiences, but changes
that delight one may surprise the other.

The shell's power rests partly on a modest process interface. It does not
need a distinct system call for every external command. It can ask the
kernel to create a process, establish file descriptors, and execute a
program. A new command installed in a searchable directory becomes part of
the user's vocabulary. The kernel need not learn its name.

\section{The pipeline as a process graph}

McIlroy repeatedly pressed the value of connecting programs. Pipes became
a kernel and shell facility early in the 1970s, after Unix had already
begun to take shape. Their arrival encouraged programs to read standard
input and write standard output in forms other programs could use.
Ritchie's evolutionary account is especially valuable because it describes
a design emerging through use, rather than attributes every later
convention to the first version \parencite[discussion of pipes]{evolution}.

Consider a deliberately simple analysis of a text file:

\begin{verbatim}
LC_ALL=C tr -cs 'A-Za-z' '\n' < notes.txt |
    tr 'A-Z' 'a-z' |
    sort |
    uniq -c |
    sort -nr
\end{verbatim}

This ASCII-oriented example counts runs of English letters after folding
case. It is not a general tokenizer for human languages. The first stage
replaces nonletter runs with newlines, the second folds uppercase, the
third places equal lines together, and the fourth counts adjacent equal
lines. The final sort orders the counts. Explicitly narrowing the problem
makes the example useful without disguising its limitations.

The vertical bar does not mean ``save the whole output, then run the next
program.'' The shell arranges a graph of processes connected by finite
kernel buffers. The stages may run concurrently. A writer can block when
a pipe is full; a reader can block when it is empty and writers remain.
This backpressure lets a pipeline process a stream larger than memory
without every stage implementing its own queueing protocol.

One consequence is easy to overlook: unused descriptors must be closed.
A reader sees end-of-file only when all write references are gone and
buffered bytes have been consumed. An accidentally inherited write end can
keep a reader waiting forever. The pipeline's attractive notation hides
real resource management, explored in \cref{ch:ipc}.

\section{A convention is a protocol}
\index{standard input}\index{standard error}

Standard input, standard output, and standard error are conventions
supported by descriptor inheritance. They are not special kinds of files
created for each command. The shell usually supplies descriptors 0, 1,
and 2 with useful meanings. A process can replace those bindings, and a
program that assumes output is always a terminal can break redirection.

Separating diagnostic messages from useful output preserves composition.
If a tool inserts progress announcements into a stream of filenames or
numbers, a downstream parser may treat them as data. Exit status is another
channel. It can distinguish ordinary completion from failure without
requiring a program to decode human prose.

The conventions are incomplete. Different tools use different status codes;
\texttt{grep}, for example, distinguishes no match from an execution error.
Historically, a shell pipeline's reported status usually came from its last
command. That can conceal an earlier failure. Some shells provide
\texttt{pipefail}, but its availability depends on shell and specification
version. A portable script must establish its target environment rather
than casually assume every useful extension exists everywhere.

Text formats are also protocols. Newline-delimited records are convenient
until a field can contain a newline. Filenames on Unix can contain spaces,
tabs, and newlines, although not a slash within a component or a NUL byte.
Parsing \texttt{ls} output is therefore a poor substitute for an interface
that passes names unambiguously. On systems that support them,
\texttt{find -print0} and \texttt{xargs -0} use NUL as a delimiter. Their
lesson is broader than their syntax: choose a representation in which data
cannot be mistaken for structure.

\section{Expansion is not just string substitution}

The shell has a distinctive evaluation model. It recognizes syntax,
performs expansions, removes quoting, and finally passes argument arrays
to programs. The program does not ordinarily receive the exact command
line the user typed. It receives separate strings after the shell has
interpreted that line.

Quoting preserves argument boundaries. If a variable contains a pathname,
\texttt{"\$name"} normally passes that value as one argument; an unquoted
expansion in traditional shells can undergo field splitting and pathname
expansion. A filename containing an asterisk may consequently become an
accidental pattern. Such bugs are not merely untidy: they can make scripts
operate on the wrong data.

Command substitution has another important property: traditional shell
syntax removes trailing newlines from the captured output. That is useful
for many textual values but means it is not a transparent transport for
arbitrary bytes. The shell is an orchestration language whose data model
fits names and commands better than unrestricted binary content.

Greenberg, Kallas, and Vasilakis argue that the shell remains worth
improving precisely because its dynamism, power, and ubiquity are hard to
replace. Their account of research on semantics and parallel execution
shows that apparent informality need not prevent rigorous analysis.
A pipeline can sometimes be transformed into a more parallel dataflow
graph, but only when the tools' behavior and side effects are sufficiently
understood \parencite{shell-future}.

\section{Editors and the growth of an environment}
\index{ed}\index{vi}\index{Emacs}\index{awk}\index{sed}\index{grep}

Editors changed alongside terminals. The line editor ed made sense when
printing a screenful of context was expensive. Its compact address and
substitution language could operate on a file without repainting a display.
Stream-oriented transformations later flourished in sed, while grep made
pattern-based selection a reusable command. These tools are related by
technique and culture, although their exact behavior and pattern languages
should not be treated as identical.

Awk added records, fields, associative arrays, and pattern-action rules.
It occupies an instructive middle ground: more structure than a bare filter,
less ceremony than a compiled application for many reporting tasks.
A task that becomes an unreadable shell tangle may become a clear awk
program because the data model better matches the work.

Vi emerged from Berkeley's screen-editing environment, with ex beneath its
visual interface. Emacs came from a different editing and Lisp tradition
and became a major part of Unix environments. Both demonstrate that Unix
users have always embraced powerful, stateful applications. Their
popularity cannot be reconciled with a literal rule that every program
must be a tiny stateless filter.

The manual, too, was part of the programming environment. Commands,
library functions, system calls, file formats, and administration procedures
could be documented in a common framework. A reference manual is not a
substitute for a tutorial, but it creates an expectation that interfaces
have names and descriptions that can be consulted locally.

\section{From improvisation to maintained automation}

A command typed once can rely on what its author happens to know about the
current directory and input. A script handed to someone else cannot.
It needs explicit assumptions about arguments, environment, working
directory, temporary files, errors, and cleanup. Interactive convenience
becomes a maintenance liability when those assumptions remain invisible.

Kernighan and Pike's treatment of the programming environment is valuable
because it connects tool use with program construction. Commands become
building blocks for experiments; successful experiments can become tools;
repeated work can move into scripts or a better-suited language. This is
a method of development rather than a claim that all software ought to be
written in shell \parencite{programming-env}.

The environment also changes the cost of asking questions. If a directory
of text files can be searched immediately, users can investigate before
building a specialized interface. If a transformation can be expressed as
four inspectable stages, an intermediate result can be checked. This is
one reason the command line survives in graphical environments: it offers
a compact language for combinations the interface designer did not predict.

The price is that users inherit responsibility for composition. A misspelled
pattern or unchecked status can invalidate a plausible-looking result.
The command line is most valuable when its operators treat each connection
as an agreement about data and failure. That discipline, more than terseness
or nostalgia, is what makes the Unix programming environment durable.
